WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.451
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.337 --> 00:00:07.170
STEPHANIE WONG: Event
Threat Detection, ETD,

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.350
is a built-in service in
Security Command Center

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:10.080
Premium.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.330
It uses log data from
Google Cloud audit logs,

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:15.300
Cloud networking logs,
and operating system logs.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.310
When a threat is detected, ETD
writes a finding to Cloud SCC

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:20.040
and to a logging project.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.500
Event Threat Detection
analyzes Cloud audit logs,

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.910
VPC flow logs, Cloud firewall
logs, Cloud NAT logs, DNS logs,

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:28.980
and Linux syslogs
to find threats.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:32.340
ETD works best when VPC flow
logging and Cloud DNS logging

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:33.445
are active.

00:00:33.445 --> 00:00:35.070
Event Threat Detection
is automatically

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.278
enabled when you subscribe
to Security Command Center

00:00:37.278 --> 00:00:38.040
Premium.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.830
If you need to
re-enable ETD, first

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:41.580
make sure you have
Security Command Center

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:44.670
Admin and Security Center
Sources Admin IAM roles,

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:47.250
along with an SCC
Premium subscription.

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:50.340
On the SCC home page, go to the
Sources and Services Settings

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:51.083
page.

00:00:51.083 --> 00:00:53.250
Then click the toggle next
to Event Threat Detection

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:54.450
to enable it.

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.100
Click Save.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.190
Then under the Resources tab,
select the scope for ETD.

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:01.860
We recommend enabling
it at the org node level

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:05.820
and allowing the rest of the
org to inherit the On setting.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.950
ETD can quickly detect
a number of potential

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:09.930
high-risk and costly threats.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:11.400
Brute force SSH.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.610
ETD detects successful
brute force of SSH on a host

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:17.460
by examining Linux auth logs
for repeated failures followed

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.380
by a successful logging.

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:20.450
Cryptomining.

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:22.800
ETD detects coin mining
malware by examining

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.430
VPC logs for connections to,
or lookup of, a known mining

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:28.250
domain or IP address.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.840
Cloud IAM abuse
malicious grants.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.450
ETD detects privileges
granted to identity and access

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:35.820
management users and service
accounts that are not

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.710
members of the organization.

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:38.610
Malware.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:41.280
ETD detects malware
based on a connection to,

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:44.340
or lookup of, a known
bad domain or IP address

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.530
by examining VPC
logs for connections

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:49.260
to known bad domains
and other log data.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:50.220
Phishing.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:53.100
ETD examines VPC logs for
suspicious connections

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.630
and other log data.

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.390
Outgoing denial of service.

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:58.530
ETD detects outgoing
denial-of-service traffic

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:02.250
by examining the sizes, types,
and numbers of VPC flow Logs.

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:04.290
Data exfiltration from BigQuery.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.600
ETD detects when data
is leaving a table owned

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.639
by your organization
or when a copy

00:02:08.639 --> 00:02:11.480
is attempted that is blocked
by a VPC Service Control.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:13.980
You can learn more about these
findings in the documentation

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:14.940
linked below.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:16.570
You can view findings
in Cloud logging

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:18.990
if you configured SCC
sinks to write logs

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:20.970
to the Google Cloud
operation suite

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:23.160
or in the SCC Findings tab.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:26.400
Filter by source type and
select Event Threat Detection.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.740
In our environment, we
can see our top findings.

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:31.710
Let's drill into the
Malware Bad IP finding.

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:35.160
We're able to see what the event
was, when the event occurred,

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:38.390
the source of the finding data,
and the detection severity.

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:41.468
Under Source Properties, we
can find additional evidence.

00:02:41.468 --> 00:02:43.260
Drilling into the
evidence, we can find out

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:47.970
the log ID, project,
timestamps, and insert ID.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:49.770
We can also see the
affected resources.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.510
And under Properties,
we can even

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:54.570
see the source IP,
port, destination IP,

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:56.215
and instance details.

00:02:56.215 --> 00:02:58.590
To display all findings that
were affected by this source

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:00.900
IP, we can apply a filter.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:02.940
In the Findings tab
filter box, enter

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:07.680
sourceProperties properties
ips, and paste the source IP.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:09.240
Security Command
Center displays all

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:11.640
of the findings that are
associated with event findings

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:13.740
affected by this source IP.

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:16.530
Next, we can continue to
pivot on either this IP

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:19.290
or the project in the filter
bar to do further detections

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:22.380
and create firewall rules
blocking access from this IP.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:23.268
Congratulations.

00:03:23.268 --> 00:03:24.810
You've enabled Event
Threat Detection

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.090
and learned how to respond
to a finding from it.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:30.740
[MUSIC PLAYING]

